INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A quality auditor calculates ''PPM'' (parts per million defective) for a supplier across 6 shipments: 2,100 / 3,400 / 1,800 / 4,200 / 2,900 / 2,600 (each figure already normalized to PPM). The supplier claims their ''average PPM is under 3,000, well within our 3,500 target.'' Verify this claim, and identify what additional insight the RAW data reveals that the simple average conceals.', '["Average = (2,100+3,400+1,800+4,200+2,900+2,600)/6 = 17,000/6 ≈ 2,833 PPM — the average claim is technically correct (under 3,000), BUT one individual shipment (4,200 PPM) exceeded the 3,500 target significantly, meaning the average conceals real shipment-to-shipment INCONSISTENCY that a pure average-based claim doesn''t reveal — a quality manager should investigate this specific high-PPM shipment rather than accepting the reassuring average alone","Average = 3,000 PPM exactly, meaning the supplier''s claim is technically false since it doesn''t meet their own stated ''under 3,000'' claim, with no other insight to extract from the individual data points","All six individual shipments were within the 3,500 target with zero exceedance, making the supplier''s claim both accurate and complete with no additional insight needed from the raw data","PPM data cannot be meaningfully averaged across different shipments, making the supplier''s average-based claim technically invalid regardless of the actual calculated result"]'::jsonb, 'Average = (2,100+3,400+1,800+4,200+2,900+2,600)/6 = 17,000/6 ≈ 2,833 PPM — the average claim is technically correct (under 3,000), BUT one individual shipment (4,200 PPM) exceeded the 3,500 target significantly, meaning the average conceals real shipment-to-shipment INCONSISTENCY that a pure average-based claim doesn''t reveal — a quality manager should investigate this specific high-PPM shipment rather than accepting the reassuring average alone', 'Average PPM = 17,000/6 ≈ 2,833, technically supporting the supplier''s ''under 3,000'' claim — but this directly connects to the earlier defect-severity-pattern-analysis principle: the AVERAGE conceals that ONE shipment (4,200 PPM) meaningfully EXCEEDED the 3,500 target, representing a real quality inconsistency/risk event that a purely average-based summary claim doesn''t surface — a diligent quality manager should look at the shipment-by-shipment DISTRIBUTION (not just the average) and specifically investigate what happened with that one high-PPM shipment, rather than being reassured by an accurate-but-incomplete average-based supplier claim.', 3, '{"PPM-average-vs-distribution"}'::text[]),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A textile testing lab''s ISO 17025 accreditation scope explicitly lists specific test methods it''s accredited for (e.g., ''ISO 13934-1 Tensile Strength'' and ''ISO 105-C06 Colorfastness to Washing'') but does NOT list ''AATCC 8 Crocking.'' If this lab issues a report showing AATCC 8 Crocking results WITHOUT clearly disclosing that this specific test is outside their accredited scope, what is the genuine concern?', '["No concern exists; ISO 17025 accreditation for SOME test methods automatically implies competence and accreditation for ANY other test method the lab happens to also perform, making scope-specific disclosure unnecessary","ISO 17025 accreditation is method-SPECIFIC, not a blanket certification of the lab''s overall competence — a result for a NON-accredited method (even from an otherwise-accredited lab) hasn''t been independently verified to meet the same rigorous quality/competence standard as the lab''s accredited methods, so presenting it without clear disclosure could mislead a buyer into extending the same trust/confidence to a result that hasn''t actually earned that same level of independent verification","AATCC and ISO test methods are always functionally identical to each other, making the specific accreditation-scope distinction between them technically meaningless","A lab''s ISO 17025 accreditation status has no relationship to how much a buyer should trust any specific test result, making the accredited-vs-non-accredited scope distinction entirely irrelevant to result interpretation"]'::jsonb, 'ISO 17025 accreditation is method-SPECIFIC, not a blanket certification of the lab''s overall competence — a result for a NON-accredited method (even from an otherwise-accredited lab) hasn''t been independently verified to meet the same rigorous quality/competence standard as the lab''s accredited methods, so presenting it without clear disclosure could mislead a buyer into extending the same trust/confidence to a result that hasn''t actually earned that same level of independent verification', 'This directly extends the earlier ISO 17025 lab-accreditation discussion with a critical, method-specific nuance — accreditation certifies a lab''s competence for SPECIFIC listed test methods, not a general blanket endorsement of everything that lab does — a result for a method OUTSIDE the accredited scope (even performed by an otherwise well-regarded, accredited lab) hasn''t undergone the same independent verification of testing competence for THAT specific method, so presenting such a result without clearly flagging its non-accredited status risks a buyer mistakenly extending the same trust earned by the lab''s accredited methods to a result that hasn''t actually earned that same verified confidence level — which is exactly why buyers should always check a lab''s SPECIFIC accreditation scope against the SPECIFIC test method being reported, not just the lab''s general accreditation status.', 3, '{"ISO-17025-scope-specificity"}'::text[]),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A quality department applies ''FMEA'' (Failure Mode and Effects Analysis — systematically scoring each potential failure mode by Severity × Occurrence × Detection to calculate a Risk Priority Number, RPN, then prioritizing corrective action on the highest-RPN items) to a new garment style''s production process. One failure mode scores Severity=9, Occurrence=2, Detection=8 (RPN=144); another scores Severity=3, Occurrence=8, Detection=6 (RPN=144). Both have IDENTICAL RPN, but should they be treated identically in terms of prioritization?', '["Yes, always — identical RPN scores always mean identical prioritization treatment is appropriate, with zero need to consider the individual Severity/Occurrence/Detection components separately","Not necessarily — even with identical total RPN, the HIGH-SEVERITY, low-occurrence failure mode (Severity=9) represents a RARE but potentially CATASTROPHIC failure when it does occur, while the LOW-SEVERITY, high-occurrence failure mode (Occurrence=8) represents a FREQUENT but relatively minor issue — many quality frameworks specifically flag HIGH SEVERITY scores (regardless of the overall RPN) for priority attention EVEN IF the overall RPN is tied with a lower-severity item, since a catastrophic-but-rare failure can carry disproportionate real-world consequence (e.g., safety or major compliance failure) that the multiplied RPN number alone doesn''t fully capture","RPN is calculated using ADDITION (not multiplication) of Severity, Occurrence, and Detection, making the described 9×2×8=144 and 3×8×6=144 calculations both mathematically incorrect","FMEA analysis has no relationship to actual production quality prioritization decisions, making this entire framework technically irrelevant to real garment manufacturing quality management"]'::jsonb, 'Not necessarily — even with identical total RPN, the HIGH-SEVERITY, low-occurrence failure mode (Severity=9) represents a RARE but potentially CATASTROPHIC failure when it does occur, while the LOW-SEVERITY, high-occurrence failure mode (Occurrence=8) represents a FREQUENT but relatively minor issue — many quality frameworks specifically flag HIGH SEVERITY scores (regardless of the overall RPN) for priority attention EVEN IF the overall RPN is tied with a lower-severity item, since a catastrophic-but-rare failure can carry disproportionate real-world consequence (e.g., safety or major compliance failure) that the multiplied RPN number alone doesn''t fully capture', 'This is a genuinely important, sophisticated FMEA interpretation nuance — while RPN (Severity×Occurrence×Detection) is a useful single composite prioritization number, identical RPN scores can mask very different underlying RISK PROFILES: a high-severity/low-occurrence failure mode represents a rare but potentially catastrophic outcome (e.g., a safety hazard or critical compliance failure) when it DOES occur, while a low-severity/high-occurrence mode represents a frequent but individually minor nuisance — many mature quality frameworks specifically add a SEVERITY-based override rule (flagging any failure mode above a certain severity threshold for priority attention regardless of its calculated RPN), precisely because a single multiplied number can obscure this critical risk-PROFILE distinction, directly echoing the earlier lesson about single blended metrics sometimes concealing important underlying distribution/severity information.', 3, '{"FMEA-RPN-severity-override"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
